  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather today through late this\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA weak cold front will move through the area early this morning.\nWinds veer around to the north as it moves through with very\nlittle cloud cover expected with fropa. Moisture pools ahead of\nthe front with possible fog developing over Downeast this\nmorning. Nighttime microphysics imagery and area webcams show\npatchy fog beginning to develop as of 04z. \n\nMax temps will be some 10 degrees below yda/s values over the\nnorth with similar temps over southern areas. Highs will range\nfrom around 50 over the north to around 60 south. Clear skies\nand calms winds for most of the area tonight with mins dropping\nblo frzg everywhere. Cannot rule out a low stratus deck moving\ninto the North Woods after midnight tonight and thru daybreak on\nWednesday morning. \n\nAn extended period of dry weather is expected through the end of\nthe week under extremely dry airmass. Noreaster continues to \nspin off of the mid-Atlantic coast before exiting to the east \nThu into Fri. Some discrepancies in model guidance is beginning \nto show with EC closing off upr low over the northeast by the \nend of the week and bringing pcpn into the area Thursday night \nand has been hinting at this soln for the past 2 days. 12z \nCanadian is also beginning to pick up on this soln as is the \nICON and JMA for what that/s worth. All this to say that confidence\nis lower in no precipitation expected through the end of the \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Nor'easter could potentially cause minor beach \nerosion to the coast from tonight into possibly Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cutoff low off the Mid Atlantic Coast lifts Wednesday morning\nnortheast through Thursday morning, then either continues \nnortheast into The Maritimes or heads eastward Friday.\n\nEnsemble guidance generally keeps the storm well offshore but a\nfew solutions bring it further north towards our area. If the \nstronger more northern solutions for this Nor'easter are \ncorrect, then there could be some minor beach erosion along \nportions of the Downeast coast around the time of high tide from\ntonight into at least Thursday. While predicted water \nlevels will remain below coastal flood benchmarks, there could \nbe sufficient wave setup/runup over multiple high tide cycles to\ncause some minor beach erosion at more vulnerable locations \nalong the coasts of Hancock and Washington Counties. With the vast\nmajority of solutions with the Nor'easter not favoring a \nstronger and more northward track at this time, the current \nchance for even minor beach erosion with this system is very \nlow, at 10-20%.",
